ets  don't fall into step with parts of the  u.s they are growing  i feel they're so it's smaller but i  feel like the community sense is  stronger  for 21 years foosh has been part of that  community  our main goal was to bring stuff to  edmonton that  nobody else was bringing in at one point  justin durr owned more than one  thousand pairs i was wearing artwork on  my feet  and the growing interest is keeping his  business alive  when so many others are struggling there  have been challenges  large conventions have been canceled and  usually when an exclusive and limited  edition shoe is released  people show up hours before the store  opens to get their foot  in the door having the lineups it allows  us to interact with the people that are  buying the shoes  and that's something that we're  passionate about obviously the shoes  themselves but it's really nice to talk  to somebody  about the product and maybe learn about  their history collecting  what got them into it still sneakerheads  are getting creative  rhodas customizes his sneakers now the  local teacher using it as a mix of an  art lesson  and history class they're always asking  me oh like when can you paint my shoes  next or  can you and i said well if you do really  good in the semester or in this grade  then maybe i might  uh we went work out something or just  something right to motivate them  for anyone wanting their own lesson in  collecting these experts say  just like wine vintage is better do your  research i mean there's so many  ways you can get to know the history  behind the designs  a hobby you can put your own stamp on  that can bring in some  serious coin how much do these go for  mac oh  too much probably  what brianna carson smith global news  and that is global national for this  sunday i'm colleen christie tonight's  your canada  is the picturesque mont santeller in  quebec if you'd like to share your  canada with us please email your photos  to viewers at globalnational.com  thanks 